VoxKeyPaste was inspired by WhisperWriter (https://github.com/savbell/whisper-writer,
GPL-3.0) by savbell. It is an independent implementation written from scratch and
contains no code from WhisperWriter.

The built-in sounds (voxkeypaste/assets/*.wav) are synthesised by tools/make_sounds.py
and the icons by tools/make_icons.py; both are part of this project (MIT).

Third-party software
--------------------
VoxKeyPaste does not include third-party code in this repository. At install time it
uses the following packages, each under its own licence:

  PySide6-Essentials (Qt for Python)  LGPL-3.0-only / GPL-2.0 / GPL-3.0   https://pyside.org
  pynput                               LGPL-3.0                            https://github.com/moses-palmer/pynput
  faster-whisper                       MIT                                 https://github.com/SYSTRAN/faster-whisper
  ctranslate2                          MIT                                 https://github.com/OpenNMT/CTranslate2
  onnxruntime                          MIT                                 https://onnxruntime.ai
  huggingface-hub                      Apache-2.0                          https://github.com/huggingface/huggingface_hub
  tokenizers                           Apache-2.0                          https://github.com/huggingface/tokenizers
  tqdm                                 MPL-2.0 AND MIT                     https://tqdm.github.io
  numpy                             BSD-3-Clause                        https://numpy.org
  sounddevice (bundles PortAudio, MIT) MIT                                 https://github.com/spatialaudio/python-sounddevice
  soundfile (bundles libsndfile, LGPL) BSD-3-Clause                        https://github.com/bastibe/python-soundfile
  pyperclip                            BSD-3-Clause                        https://github.com/asweigart/pyperclip
  webrtcvad-wheels                     MIT                                 https://github.com/daanzu/py-webrtcvad-wheels
  httpx                                BSD-3-Clause                        https://www.python-httpx.org
  PyYAML                               MIT                                 https://pyyaml.org
  pycaw (Windows)                      MIT                                 https://github.com/AndreMiras/pycaw
  comtypes (Windows)                   MIT                                 https://github.com/enthought/comtypes

pip installs these along with them, as their dependencies:

  certifi                              MPL-2.0                             https://github.com/certifi/python-certifi
  shiboken6 (part of PySide6)          LGPL-3.0-only / GPL-2.0 / GPL-3.0   https://pyside.org

  MIT:          anyio, filelock, h11, six
  MIT-0:        cffi
  BSD:          click, colorama, fsspec, httpcore, idna, protobuf, psutil, pycparser
  Apache-2.0:   flatbuffers, hf-xet
  Dual:         packaging (Apache-2.0 OR BSD-2-Clause)
  PSF-2.0:      typing-extensions

faster-whisper is installed without its own dependencies (pip's --no-deps), and the ones
it needs are listed above. That leaves out PyAV, which it asks for and VoxKeyPaste does not
need. PyAV is optional and not installed by default, only with requirements-av.txt:

  av (PyAV)                            BSD-3-Clause                        https://github.com/PyAV-Org/PyAV
    Its wheels bring FFmpeg (LGPL-3.0-or-later) and the video encoders libx264 and
    libx265 (GPL-2.0-or-later). Install it only knowingly.

The ctranslate2 wheel for Windows also carries cudnn64_9.dll, the NVIDIA cuDNN Library, which
is NVIDIA's own software and not under ctranslate2's MIT License. VoxKeyPaste does not ship
it: pip downloads it from PyPI along with ctranslate2.

The Whisper models downloaded by faster-whisper are released by OpenAI under the MIT
License (https://github.com/openai/whisper).

PySide6 and pynput are used as unmodified, dynamically imported libraries, as the LGPL
allows; you can replace them with any compatible version. The same goes for certifi and
tqdm under the MPL.

Development only (requirements-dev.txt, never installed with the app): pytest (MIT),
pluggy (MIT), iniconfig (MIT), Pygments (BSD-2-Clause) and Pillow (MIT-CMU).
